WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:02.000
-Come on, Cameron.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.367
Oh, we love you.
-Jimmy.

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:04.834
-Come on.
-Come on.

00:00:04.834 --> 00:00:08.100
-Look at this. I know it's not
100,000 people, but, come on.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.300
This feels like --
It feels like it, right?

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:17.267
-I feel the love. Thank you.
My heart is beating very fast.

00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:19.167
I don't, like,
walk into crowds of people

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.067
and have them applaud for me
very often. I'm like, "What?"

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.167
-We were just talking
backstage about your husband,

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:26.701
who's superstar rock star

00:00:26.701 --> 00:00:28.534
from Good Charlotte,
Benji Madden.

00:00:28.534 --> 00:00:29.634
-Yeah.
-Whoo!

00:00:29.634 --> 00:00:31.267
-But he just played --
Yeah -- oww!

00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:32.667
He, uh --
[ Laughter ]

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:34.467
Well, he just played --
Was it last weekend?

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:35.634
-Last weekend.

00:00:35.634 --> 00:00:38.067
And he did the
When We Were Young Festival.

00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:40.834
It's, like, the pop-punk
festival that was in Las Vegas.

00:00:40.834 --> 00:00:42.801
-Link is there and Green Day.
-Yeah, Green Day.

00:00:42.801 --> 00:00:45.901
And it was like this whole,
like, sea of people.

00:00:45.901 --> 00:00:49.000
First off, Good Charlotte hasn't
played out in like five years,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.200
they haven't done a show.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.167
So here they are, like,
up on the stage,

00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:53.601
100,000 people just, like,

00:00:53.601 --> 00:00:55.667
singing every word
to their song,

00:00:55.667 --> 00:00:57.901
jumping all at the same time.
It was, like, so wild.

00:00:57.901 --> 00:01:00.334
I was like, "Oh, yeah, right.
You do that, too."

00:01:00.334 --> 00:01:03.501
-Yeah, yeah.
"You're a giant rock star."

00:01:03.501 --> 00:01:05.267
-Exactly. He's like, you know,
Benj at home

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:07.100
with being the best dad ever

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.434
and running
all of his businesses,

00:01:08.434 --> 00:01:09.567
and then he just gets up there,
and he's like,

00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:11.334
"Yeah, all right, pyro."
Pfft!

00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:12.801
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:15.200
-When he's at home, does he do
songs for your daughter or...?

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.334
-Yeah, he makes up --
He has, like, honestly,

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:18.467
I'm like,"Why don't we just --

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:21.767
You need to do a --
like, a kids album,"

00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:24.100
because he has, like,
the best songs.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.801
They play in my head
all the time

00:01:25.801 --> 00:01:28.601
because he sings them to her,
and she knows the words,

00:01:28.601 --> 00:01:30.133
and it's the sweetest
thing ever.

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:32.367
It really is.
It's so -- it kills me.

00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:36.033
-I don't think I've ever
written a song for my daugh--

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:38.000
-You haven't?
-No, I tried.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.467
Everyone thinks I should.
-He has some bangers.

00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:41.467
I'm telling you.
I'm like, "Whoa.

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:42.968
That is, like -- That's catchy.

00:01:42.968 --> 00:01:45.501
-Where was he
when "Baby Shark" existed?

00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:47.634
I needed his help really bad.
Yeah.

00:01:47.634 --> 00:01:50.100
-Catatonic, like, shaking,
like, rocking in the corner.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.501
-I mean, the "Paw Patrol" theme
was big in our house.

00:01:52.501 --> 00:01:54.067
Like, all that stuff.
-Oh, yeah.

00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:56.534
But basically, "Paw Patrol"
is just like a pop-punk song.

00:01:56.534 --> 00:01:58.634
-It really is.
-♪ Paw Patrol, Paw Patrol ♪

00:01:58.634 --> 00:02:00.501
♪ Be there on the double ♪

00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:02.767
-Yeah. You can --
-That sounds really familiar,

00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:04.000
Good Charlotte fans.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.234
-They should have done that.
They need my help.

00:02:06.234 --> 00:02:09.667
The next tour,
I can give him a list.

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:12.634
I also saw, by the way,
you went and saw the Eras tour.

00:02:12.634 --> 00:02:13.801
-I did, yes.
-Which...

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:17.734
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yes.

00:02:17.734 --> 00:02:19.501
-I heard it was amazing.
-It was amazing.

00:02:19.501 --> 00:02:21.234
I mean, you're like,
"Okay, there you go,"

00:02:21.234 --> 00:02:23.300
for three hours, okay.
-And -- Yeah.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.701
And she just does it.
And it feels like 20 minutes --

00:02:25.701 --> 00:02:27.767
like 20 minutes.
Here's you and the crew here.

00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:28.901
-This is my girls.
Okay.

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:30.868
So, Zoe Saldana is, like,
one of my besties,

00:02:30.868 --> 00:02:33.367
and she was like -- we were
talking the night before,

00:02:33.367 --> 00:02:35.067
and she was like, "Do you --

00:02:35.067 --> 00:02:37.334
Should we go see,
like, Taylor Swift?"

00:02:37.334 --> 00:02:38.801
and I was like, "Yeah,
I think we should,

00:02:38.801 --> 00:02:40.767
but how are we ever
going to get tickets?"

00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:45.167
Then, Elsa Collins calls Zoe the
next morning and says,

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:48.167
"Let's go," and we show up,
and Tiffany is there,

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:50.167
who is, like,
the biggest Swiftie ever.

00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:51.501
-Tiffany Haddish
is a big Swiftie?

00:02:51.501 --> 00:02:54.701
-Yes! She's like, "I love --
I love this girl."

00:02:54.701 --> 00:02:57.634
She's like --
she just goes in on every word.

00:02:57.634 --> 00:02:59.434
She knows everything.
-Tiffany Haddish.

00:02:59.434 --> 00:03:00.634
-Yeah.
-Really?

00:03:00.634 --> 00:03:02.300
I didn't think
of her as a giant Swiftie.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.300
-You know what?
It's a good time,

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:05.968
watching a --
watching a show with Tiffany.

00:03:05.968 --> 00:03:07.801
-It's probably a good time doing
anything with Tiffany Haddish.

00:03:07.801 --> 00:03:10.334
-Anything.
She loves tea, I learned.

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:12.033
Like,
she's a tea connoisseur.

00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:13.267
-Really?
-I'm just like,

00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:15.734
"Will you please invite me over
for some tea,

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:18.067
and then we can spill the tea?"

00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:20.634
-Yes, of course we can
spill the tea, yeah.

00:03:20.634 --> 00:03:23.267
I mean, I want to talk to you
about the wine.

00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:26.067
I want to talk about Avaline.
But I also want to talk about --

00:03:26.067 --> 00:03:27.834
You did this thing
with Goop over the summer

00:03:27.834 --> 00:03:29.133
where you had
your own signature --

00:03:29.133 --> 00:03:30.467
signature salad.
-Yeah.

00:03:30.467 --> 00:03:32.534
-And I know that you can cook.

00:03:32.534 --> 00:03:34.133
-I like food.
-Yes, but --

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:36.367
-Food's good.
-How did you get into cooking?

00:03:36.367 --> 00:03:37.868
Because since I've known you,
you've always cooked.

00:03:37.868 --> 00:03:40.534
-I just cook with my mom.
Every night, I cook dinner

00:03:40.534 --> 00:03:42.634
with my mom.
And me and my mom still, like,

00:03:42.634 --> 00:03:46.033
cook together on the phone,
like FaceTime on the weekends.

00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:47.934
Like, I'll cook -- cook her --
-No way.

00:03:47.934 --> 00:03:49.634
-I'm like,
"Let's cook lunch together,"

00:03:49.634 --> 00:03:51.234
and we'll, like,
get on the phone

00:03:51.234 --> 00:03:53.901
and FaceTime and cook and talk
and hang out

00:03:53.901 --> 00:03:56.434
while she's cooking her lunch.
I'm cooking my lunch.

00:03:56.434 --> 00:03:58.534
-Would you ever -- is there --
is there a cooking thing

00:03:58.534 --> 00:04:00.601
coming out in the future
for Cameron Diaz?

00:04:00.601 --> 00:04:03.033
-I don't know.
It could be.

00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:04.901
I mean, all my friends say
that I need to,

00:04:04.901 --> 00:04:09.100
like, write a book, a cookbook
and call it "Put an Egg On It."

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.234
Because every time, I'm like,
"You just -- You know what?

00:04:11.234 --> 00:04:13.801
That's really good. It's really
good with an egg on it.

00:04:13.801 --> 00:04:15.834
Just put an egg on it.
Put -- put an egg on it."

00:04:15.834 --> 00:04:16.868
I'm really -- yeah.

00:04:16.868 --> 00:04:19.167
I'm the queen of morning hash.

00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:21.167
Like, I'll take everything
out of the refrigerator

00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:23.634
and, like, chop it all up
and, like, layer it,

00:04:23.634 --> 00:04:25.234
and then I'm, like -- fwt!
Pmf!

00:04:25.234 --> 00:04:26.968
-Put an egg on it! Come on!
-Like, "Put an egg on it,

00:04:26.968 --> 00:04:28.033
Call it breakfast.
-Put an egg on it!

00:04:28.033 --> 00:04:29.834
-You know?
-Can we talk about --

00:04:29.834 --> 00:04:31.367
Let's talk about Avaline
for a bit.

00:04:31.367 --> 00:04:33.567
This is a -- this is fantastic,
by the way.

00:04:33.567 --> 00:04:35.634
This is one of
the fastest-growing wine

00:04:35.634 --> 00:04:37.567
brands in the country.
-Yeah.

00:04:37.567 --> 00:04:39.801
-Now, when you first started
this, you came on the show.

00:04:39.801 --> 00:04:41.300
Thank you very much.
-Yeah. Thank you.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.767
-And you were like, "Yeah, we're
just hoping to get this message

00:04:43.767 --> 00:04:45.901
out and kind of disrupt
the wine industry

00:04:45.901 --> 00:04:47.901
a little bit and teach people
how to do it the right way.

00:04:47.901 --> 00:04:48.968
-Yeah.
-Now you're one of the leaders

00:04:48.968 --> 00:04:50.434
in this category.
-Yeah.

00:04:50.434 --> 00:04:53.000
We're really excited
about Avaline being able

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.234
to provide
exactly what we wanted,

00:04:55.234 --> 00:04:58.501
which was total transparency
in winemaking process.

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:01.100
What's in the -- letting people
know what's in the bottle

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.734
and what's not in the bottle.

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:08.234
And so we have multiple SKUs
or varietals and blends.

00:05:08.234 --> 00:05:09.701
And this is our newest,
the Pinot Noir,

00:05:09.701 --> 00:05:12.834
which is on our website,
drinkavaline.com.

00:05:12.834 --> 00:05:15.801
And it's so --
it's the most delicious wine.

00:05:15.801 --> 00:05:17.267
It's so good for the holidays.

00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:20.701
And we also have
a holiday limited edition

00:05:20.701 --> 00:05:22.901
of all Italian wines.
Like, there's --

00:05:22.901 --> 00:05:27.501
It's Sangiovese,
we have a Pinot Grigio,

00:05:27.501 --> 00:05:30.834
a Lambrusco,
which is, like, so delicious.

00:05:30.834 --> 00:05:32.534
-Get in there now before --
You got to order

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:33.968
before the holidays.
Yeah, come.

00:05:33.968 --> 00:05:35.367
You'll need it for, yeah,
Thanksgiving, yeah.

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:37.834
-It's good for everything.
-Friendsgiving. Yeah, exactly.

00:05:37.834 --> 00:05:39.367
-Friendsgiving, exactly.
-Yeah, you need it.

00:05:39.367 --> 00:05:41.400
-And great gifts. Great gifts.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.868
Because that's, like,
literally why I started --

00:05:43.868 --> 00:05:45.601
one of the reasons
why I started my wine company

00:05:45.601 --> 00:05:48.501
is because all I ever do
for gifting is, like,

00:05:48.501 --> 00:05:51.067
just give a bottle of wine
because I want people to enjoy.

00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:53.367
I know what they want to --

00:05:53.367 --> 00:05:54.901
I know they're
going to drink it.

00:05:54.901 --> 00:05:57.567
So now I just, you know --
I give my own wine as a gift.

00:05:57.567 --> 00:05:59.968
-I love that you get --
you're involved with this,

00:05:59.968 --> 00:06:02.501
every little thing,
down to the color of the label,

00:06:02.501 --> 00:06:04.367
down to the little details
that you write.

00:06:04.367 --> 00:06:05.801
A lot of wines,
you go like,

00:06:05.801 --> 00:06:09.567
"Oh, it has a taste of --
it's a fruitiness. It has --"

00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:12.634
You describe this as, like --
You go,

00:06:12.634 --> 00:06:14.467
"It pairs well with..."
and you go,

00:06:14.467 --> 00:06:15.701
"Oh, what does it
pair well with?"

00:06:15.701 --> 00:06:18.367
Food or fish
or chicken or steak.

00:06:18.367 --> 00:06:20.400
It pairs well
with fading sunlight

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.300
and your favorite song.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.501
And I love that.
-It's a lifestyle.

00:06:25.501 --> 00:06:27.234
-No, I love that.
It's so creative and fun.

00:06:27.234 --> 00:06:28.501
And why not?
-Right?

00:06:28.501 --> 00:06:30.567
-Do something different.
I thought that would be fun,

00:06:30.567 --> 00:06:32.267
so I thought
maybe we'd cheers it.

00:06:32.267 --> 00:06:35.367
Can we get a fading sunlight?
-Oh.

00:06:35.367 --> 00:06:36.767
Let's see if we can do this.

00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:38.000
[ Audience "Oohs" ]
-We're like the --

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.567
-It's like a real, like sunset.
Tuscan sunset.

00:06:40.567 --> 00:06:43.400
-We're at The Sphere!
We're live from The Sphere!

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:44.801
[ Both laugh ]

00:06:44.801 --> 00:06:47.267
We're at The Sphere
in Las Vegas, you guys!

00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:50.834
Yeah. And I was thinking, what's
my favorite song right now?

00:06:50.834 --> 00:06:52.367
Of course I'm gonna do
Good Charlotte,

00:06:52.367 --> 00:06:55.901
but instead
I'm weirdly into

00:06:55.901 --> 00:06:58.100
"Something About You,"
by Level 42.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.200
-Oh, okay.
-You remember that jam?

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:04.000
♪♪

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.133
-Oh, cheers, pal!

00:07:06.133 --> 00:07:09.567
♪♪

00:07:09.567 --> 00:07:11.501
Ooh, by the way, yummy!
-Yes!

00:07:11.501 --> 00:07:14.868
-Whoa! That's fantastic.
Cameron Diaz, everybody.

00:07:14.868 --> 00:07:18.801
Avaline is available now
at drinkavaline.com.

